README.math
===========

ixemul.library v80 - selecting and using mathematical functions
Revision 1.3  2026/09/08


1. PURPOSE AND SCOPE
--------------------

ixemul's current <math.h> has two header paths for mathematical functions:

  1. External function declarations, normally resolved through libm.
  2. Static inline FPU functions from <math-68881.h>.

The compiler options used for the application determine which header path
is selected.  The CPU option and the FPU option are separate.  In
particular, -m68060 by itself does not select <math-68881.h>.

This document describes the behaviour verified in the current ixemul
headers and how an application selects between the two paths.

The original ixemul <math.h> uses the same basic either/or design: when
__HAVE_68881__ is defined it includes <math-68881.h>; otherwise it exposes
the normal external math prototypes.  The current v80 header has retained
that historical structure.

It does NOT assume that any particular separately supplied libm source
tree, Makefile or archive layout is the standard ADE/GG libm build unless
that has been verified independently.


2. NORMAL USE
-------------

Programs should include:

    #include <math.h>

and link with libm when external mathematical functions may be needed:

    gcc -O2 -m68060 program.c -lm -o program

With a traditional static linker, place -lm after the object files or
source file that references the mathematical functions.


3. CPU OPTION VERSUS FPU OPTION
-------------------------------

Example:

    -m68060

selects code generation for the 68060 CPU, but does not by itself enable
the 68881-compatible FPU header path in ixemul's <math.h>.

Example:

    -m68060 -m68881

selects 68060 CPU code generation and enables GCC's 68881-compatible FPU
support.  In the GCC 2.95.3 environment tested for ixemul, -m68881 defines
__HAVE_68881__.  ixemul's <math.h> then includes <math-68881.h>.

The same header-selection principle applies to combinations such as:

    -m68020 -m68881
    -m68040 -m68881
    -m68060 -m68881

For ixemul's current <math.h>, __HAVE_68881__ is the relevant condition
for selecting <math-68881.h>.  CPU selection alone is not the same thing
as FPU selection.


4. EXTERNAL LIBM HEADER PATH
----------------------------

Without __HAVE_68881__, the current ixemul <math.h> declares normal
external functions including:

    acos      asin      atan      atan2
    ceil      cos       cosh      exp
    fabs      floor     fmod      frexp
    ldexp     log       log10     modf
    pow       sin       sinh      sqrt
    tan       tanh

When neither _ANSI_SOURCE nor _POSIX_SOURCE restricts the interface,
additional declarations include:

    acosh     asinh     atanh     cabs
    cbrt      copysign  drem      erf
    erfc      expm1     finite    hypot
    j0        j1        jn        lgamma
    log1p     logb      rint      scalb
    y0        y1        yn

These are declarations.  A declaration in <math.h> does not by itself
prove which external library provides the symbol, which implementation is
used, or how that library was compiled.

External availability must be verified against the actual libm installed
or selected for the build.


5. INLINE 68881/FPU HEADER PATH
-------------------------------

When GCC or C++ is used and __HAVE_68881__ is defined, the current ixemul
<math.h> includes <math-68881.h> instead of the normal external prototype
block.

This is not a v80 invention.  The original ixemul <math.h> already used
this same selection model, and the original 1989/1991 <math-68881.h>
contained its own static inline FPU implementations.

The current <math-68881.h> contains static inline implementations of:

    sin       cos       tan       asin
    acos      atan      atan2     sinh
    cosh      tanh      atanh     exp
    expf      exp2      exp2f     expm1
    log       log2      log2f     log1p
    log10     log10f    sqrt      hypot
    pow       fabs      ceil      floor
    roundf    rint      fmod      drem
    scalb     logb      ldexp     frexp
    modf      truncf

These definitions are compiled into each translation unit that uses this
header path.  They do NOT by themselves create exported symbols in an
external libm archive.

Some functions use FPU instructions directly.  Others are wrappers around
existing inline functions.  For example, the current implementation uses:

    log2(x) = log(x) * 1.4426950408889634074

and:

    exp2(x) = exp(x * 0.69314718055994530942)

Thus the current inline log2() and exp2() reuse inline log() and exp()
instead of directly adding flog2 or ftwotox assembler operations.


6. IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THE TWO PATHS
---------------------------------------------

The original and current <math.h> effectively select:

    if __HAVE_68881__
        include <math-68881.h>
    else
        declare the external math interface

This is the historical ixemul design.  It is a strict header-level
either/or choice; the two sets are not automatically merged.

A function added only to <math-68881.h> may be available when compiling
with -m68881 but still have no external symbol for a build that uses the
normal external path.

Conversely, an external function can exist in a libm archive while its
prototype is hidden by the -m68881 header path if that function is not
also exposed by <math-68881.h>.

Adding -lm does not override or merge this header choice.  -m68881 affects
preprocessing and compilation; -lm affects the later link stage.  -lm can
resolve an external symbol only if the compiled code contains an external
reference to it.  It does not make the prototypes from the skipped
<math.h> branch visible.

The original design therefore intentionally favours the inline FPU header
as a self-contained interface when __HAVE_68881__ is selected.

This distinction is especially important when adding C99 functions.


7. CURRENT C99-STYLE ADDITIONS IN MATH-68881.H
----------------------------------------------

The current <math-68881.h> contains these newer additions:

    expf
    exp2
    exp2f
    log2
    log2f
    log10f
    roundf
    truncf

Their presence in <math-68881.h> proves only that they exist as static
inline implementations on the selected FPU header path.

It must NOT be assumed from that fact alone that matching external symbols
exist in the libm used by ADE/GG or by any particular ixemul installation.

log2() demonstrated this distinction during the Lua 5.4 work: compiling
through the external path produced an unresolved reference to log2(),
while the current <math-68881.h> does contain an inline log2().

For a function that is intentionally supported on both historical paths,
the following pieces may be needed:

    FPU header path:
        static inline implementation in <math-68881.h>

    external path:
        prototype in the non-68881 branch of <math.h>
        real external implementation in the selected libm

This follows the existing either/or header design.  A different design in
which <math-68881.h> supplies inline functions and <math.h> simultaneously
supplies external prototypes for everything else would be a deliberate
modernisation, not restoration of original ixemul behaviour.


8. DO NOT INFER LIBM CONTENT FROM AN UNRELATED MAKEFILE
-------------------------------------------------------

A Makefile from a libm source tree describes only that source tree unless
it has been established that it is the libm actually used by the target
ADE/GG installation.

In particular, do not infer from such a Makefile:

    - which libm is the ADE/GG default
    - which object files are present in the installed libm
    - which CPU or FPU flags were used to build the installed libm
    - whether libm881 is a separate build or a link to another archive

Those properties must be checked against the actual installed or selected
library and its actual build system.


9. VERIFYING THE EXTERNAL LIBM
------------------------------

To determine whether a function exists externally, inspect the actual
library used for the link.

Typical checks are:

    nm /path/to/libm.a | grep log2

or, where supported:

    ar t /path/to/libm.a

The exact tools available depend on the object/archive format and the ADE
toolchain.

A successful header compile is not enough.  The final link is the decisive
test for an external function.

For example:

    #include <math.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        volatile double x = 8.0;
        volatile double y = log2(x);
        return (y == 3.0) ? 0 : 1;
    }

Compile through the external path:

    gcc -O2 -m68060 testlog2.c -lm -o testlog2

If the linker reports an undefined reference to log2, the selected
external libraries do not provide the required symbol, regardless of
whether an inline log2() exists in <math-68881.h>.


10. 68040 AND 68060 NOTE
------------------------

-m68881 tells GCC and the inline header to use the 68881-compatible FPU
instruction model.

The 68040 and 68060 have internal FPUs, but they do not implement every
68881/68882 transcendental instruction directly in hardware.  Code using
68881-style transcendental instructions may therefore depend on the
appropriate 68040/68060 floating-point support when an unsupported
instruction requires software emulation.

Do not interpret -m68881 as meaning that every FPU operation is necessarily
a native single-instruction operation on a 68040 or 68060.



11. BUILDING LIBM WITH -m68881
-----------------------------

When compiling libm itself, -m68881 has two separate effects that must not
be confused:

    1. GCC may generate 68881-compatible floating-point instructions.
    2. GCC defines __HAVE_68881__, which makes ixemul's <math.h> select
       <math-68881.h>.

-fno-inline does NOT disable the second effect.  It controls compiler
inlining, but it does not change the preprocessor selection made by
<math.h>.  Static inline definitions from <math-68881.h> are still visible
to the translation unit.

Therefore:

    -fno-inline
        does NOT mean "do not use <math-68881.h>"

If libm should be compiled with FPU code generation while avoiding the
<math-68881.h> header path, one possible GCC 2.95.3 configuration is:

    -m68881 -U__HAVE_68881__

This keeps -m68881 code generation enabled while removing the macro that
ixemul's <math.h> uses to select <math-68881.h>.

This combination must be verified with the actual GCC 2.95.3 build and the
actual libm sources before it is made part of the normal build procedure.

12. ADDING A NEW MATH FUNCTION
--------------------------------

The original ixemul design should be treated as the compatibility baseline:
the -m68881 path is supplied by <math-68881.h>, while the non-68881 path
uses external prototypes and libm.

When adding a new function such as log2(), exp2(), round() or a float
variant, decide explicitly which path or paths are intended to support it.

For support on both paths, keep the following parts synchronized where
applicable:

    A. External implementation
       Add a real implementation to the libm source tree that is actually
       used when the function must work without the inline FPU header.

    B. External library build
       Add that implementation to the real libm build system.

    C. <math.h>
       Add the external prototype in the non-68881 path.

    D. <math-68881.h>
       Add or retain a static inline implementation for the FPU path.

    E. Test both paths
       Compile and link once without -m68881 and once with -m68881.

Do not add an unconditional extern declaration for a name that is also
defined as static inline in <math-68881.h> if that would place conflicting
linkage declarations in the same translation unit.

Changing <math.h> so that the -m68881 path also receives external
prototypes for functions absent from <math-68881.h> is possible, but it is
an API/header redesign and should be evaluated separately rather than
treated as an original ixemul behaviour.


13. RECOMMENDED TEST MATRIX
---------------------------

External header/libm path:

    gcc -O2 -m68060 testmath.c -lm -o testmath

68060 inline FPU header path:

    gcc -O2 -m68060 -m68881 testmath.c -lm -o testmath-060fpu

68040 inline FPU header path:

    gcc -O2 -m68040 -m68881 testmath.c -lm -o testmath-040fpu

68020 plus 68881/68882:

    gcc -O2 -m68020 -m68881 testmath.c -lm -o testmath-020fpu

For each new function, test normal values, zero, negative-domain cases
where applicable, infinities/NaNs if supported by the implementation, and
boundary values relevant to the function.


14. DIAGNOSTICS
---------------

To verify the header path, inspect the compiler macros.

With the tested GCC 2.95.3 setup:

    gcc -m68060 -m68881 -dM -E test.c

defines:

    __HAVE_68881__

and therefore selects <math-68881.h> through ixemul's <math.h>.

Without -m68881, do not assume that header path is active.

To verify an external function, inspect and/or link against the actual libm
that the application will use.

Do not infer external availability from <math-68881.h>.  Its static inline
functions are header implementations, not proof of external archive
symbols.


15. SUMMARY
-----------

Use:

    -m68060

when you want 68060 CPU code and the normal external math header path.

Use:

    -m68060 -m68881

when you want the 68060 CPU target and ixemul's inline
68881-compatible FPU math header path.

Always remember:

    CPU selection != FPU selection

    -m68881 selects the historical inline FPU header path

    -lm does not override or merge that header selection

and:

    function present in <math-68881.h>
        !=
    function exported by the selected libm

The original ixemul design is an either/or header model.  A hybrid model
that keeps inline FPU functions while also exposing external prototypes for
missing functions would be a deliberate modernisation.

The build details of an arbitrary libm source tree must not be presented as
properties of the standard ADE/GG libm unless that relationship has been
verified.
